<p class="gna-lede">For a mild drop at the tail of the brow, or if you would like to try something without surgery, Botox (botulinum toxin) can give a small lift that lasts about three to four months. For brows that have genuinely descended and are crowding the upper eyelids, a surgical brow lift repositions the brow for many years. The right choice depends on how much lift you need, what is causing the droop and whether the real problem is actually excess eyelid skin.</p>

<h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
<p>Think of Botox as a <strong>subtle, temporary adjustment</strong> and a brow lift as a <strong>structural, longer-lasting repositioning</strong>. Botox can typically raise the outer part of the brow by a few millimetres. It cannot lift heavy, low brows, and in some people it can make a droop worse. Surgery can lift more and lasts longer, but it involves incisions, recovery time and the risks of an operation.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A "Botox brow lift" gives a small lift, mainly at the tail of the brow, for about three to four months.</li>
    <li>A surgical brow lift can raise and reshape the brow for many years, although ageing continues.</li>
    <li>Hooded eyelids are often caused by both low brows and excess eyelid skin — treating only one may disappoint.</li>
    <li>People who already use their forehead muscle to hold their brows up need careful assessment before Botox.</li>
    <li>Results and recovery vary; an in-person examination is the only way to judge which option fits.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="why-brows-droop">Why do brows droop?</h2>
<p>The brow is held up by a single muscle — the <strong>frontalis</strong>, which spans the forehead — and pulled down by several smaller muscles around the eyes and between the brows. Over time, several changes shift that balance:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Loss of skin elasticity and gradual descent of the soft tissues of the forehead and temples</li>
  <li>Loss of volume in the fat pads beneath the brow</li>
  <li>Gradual remodelling of the bone around the eye socket</li>
  <li>Inherited features — some people simply have low or heavy brows from a young age</li>
  <li>Less commonly, weakness of the facial nerve after Bell's palsy, injury or surgery, which can cause a droop on one side</li>
</ul>
<p>The outer part of the brow usually drops first, because the frontalis muscle does not extend as far as the tail of the brow and offers it less support. This can create a tired or sad look and pushes skin down over the outer part of the upper eyelid.</p>

<h2 id="brows-or-eyelids">Is it your brows or your eyelids?</h2>
<p>Many people who believe they need eyelid surgery actually have low brows, and vice versa. Often it is a mixture of both. A simple check you can try in the mirror: relax your forehead completely, then gently lift your brow with a fingertip to where you would like it to sit. If most of the hooding disappears, the brow is a major contributor. If a fold of loose skin remains on the eyelid, excess eyelid skin (dermatochalasis) is also playing a part.</p>
<p>This matters because removing eyelid skin alone when the brow is low can pull the brow down further, while lifting the brow alone may leave loose eyelid skin behind. Some patients are well served by combining a brow lift with <a href="/eyelid-surgery">eyelid surgery</a>; our <a href="/blogs/blepharoplasty-eyelid-surgery-guide/">blepharoplasty guide</a> explains that procedure in detail.</p>
<p>Another clue: if you constantly raise your eyebrows to see clearly, and your forehead lines are deep even at rest, your frontalis may be working hard to compensate for low brows or heavy lids. That has important implications for Botox, as explained below.</p>

<h2 id="botox-brow-lift">How Botox lifts the brows</h2>
<p>Botulinum toxin temporarily relaxes the muscles it is injected into. A "chemical brow lift" works by relaxing the muscles that pull the brow <em>down</em> — mainly the outer part of the circular muscle around the eye and the frown muscles between the brows — so that the frontalis can lift the brow slightly more freely.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>What it can do:</strong> a modest lift of a few millimetres, usually at the tail of the brow, and a smoother, more open look between the brows.</li>
  <li><strong>How long it lasts:</strong> the effect appears over three to fourteen days and usually lasts around three to four months, so treatment must be repeated to maintain it.</li>
  <li><strong>What it cannot do:</strong> lift heavy, significantly low brows or remove excess eyelid skin.</li>
</ul>
<p>There is a catch. If toxin is also placed in the forehead to smooth lines and the frontalis is weakened too much, the brow can <strong>drop</strong> rather than rise — and this is more likely in people who rely on the frontalis to hold up low brows or heavy lids. Poorly balanced placement can also leave an exaggerated, peaked arch. Careful assessment and conservative dosing reduce these risks. Read more on our <a href="/botox-treatment">Botox treatment</a> page.</p>

