<h2 id="surgical-brow-lift">Surgical brow lift options</h2>
<p>A surgical brow lift (also called a forehead lift) raises the brow and the soft tissues of the forehead and secures them in a higher position. Several approaches exist, and the choice depends on your hairline, forehead height, the shape of lift needed and where you are willing to have a scar.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Endoscopic brow lift:</strong> three to five short incisions behind the hairline, with a small camera used to release and lift the tissues. Suits many people with a normal or low hairline, with scars hidden in the hair.</li>
  <li><strong>Temporal (lateral) brow lift:</strong> incisions in the hair-bearing temple to lift the outer brow specifically.</li>
  <li><strong>Hairline (pretrichial) brow lift:</strong> an incision along the front of the hairline; it lifts the brow without raising a high hairline further and can shorten a long forehead.</li>
  <li><strong>Coronal brow lift:</strong> an incision across the scalp from ear to ear; it gives a powerful lift but raises the hairline and is used less often today.</li>
  <li><strong>Direct brow lift:</strong> a strip of skin is removed just above the brow, leaving a scar along the brow edge. It is often considered for older patients, men with receding hairlines, or a droop caused by facial nerve weakness.</li>
  <li><strong>Brow support through an eyelid incision (browpexy):</strong> a small adjustment made during upper eyelid surgery, offering limited lift.</li>
</ul>
<p>The result usually remains visible for many years, though the brow continues to age naturally. Most brow lifts are done under general anaesthesia or local anaesthesia with sedation, typically as a day-care procedure.</p>

<h2 id="comparison">Brow lift vs Botox at a glance</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th></th><th>Botox ("chemical brow lift")</th><th>Surgical brow lift</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Amount of lift</td><td>Small, mainly at the tail of the brow</td><td>Moderate to significant; can be shaped along the brow</td></tr>
    <tr><td>How long it lasts</td><td>About 3–4 months</td><td>Many years</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Procedure</td><td>A few injections in clinic, about 15 minutes</td><td>Surgery under anaesthesia, usually day care</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Downtime</td><td>Minimal; small bruises possible</td><td>Swelling and bruising for 1–2 weeks</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Scars</td><td>None</td><td>Yes, usually hidden in the hair or a natural line</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Main risks</td><td>Brow or eyelid droop, asymmetry, headache, bruising — all temporary</td><td>Scalp numbness, scarring, asymmetry, hair loss at the incision, nerve injury (uncommon)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Reversibility</td><td>Wears off on its own</td><td>Not easily reversed</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Typical candidate</td><td>Mild droop, younger patients, or a trial before considering surgery</td><td>Established brow descent with eyelid hooding</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="risks-recovery">Risks and recovery</h2>
<h3>After Botox</h3>
<p>Small bruises, a mild headache and tenderness at the injection sites are common and settle within days. Less commonly, the toxin spreads to the muscle that opens the upper eyelid, causing a droopy lid for several weeks, or produces an uneven or over-arched brow. These effects are temporary but can be frustrating. Avoid rubbing the area and follow any advice about lying flat or exercising for the first few hours.</p>
<h3>After a surgical brow lift</h3>
<p>Expect swelling and bruising of the forehead and around the eyes for one to two weeks; bruising can track down towards the cheeks. Keeping your head raised and using cold compresses in the first few days helps. Stitches or clips are usually removed after one to two weeks. Many people return to desk work in about ten days to two weeks, but the final shape settles over several months. Recovery varies between individuals.</p>
<p>Surgical risks include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Numbness or itching of the forehead and scalp, usually improving over months but occasionally persistent</li>
  <li>Visible scarring or hair thinning along the incision</li>
  <li>Asymmetry, or a lift that is more or less than planned, sometimes needing revision</li>
  <li>Injury to the nerve branch that raises the brow, causing weakness — uncommon and usually temporary</li>
  <li>Bleeding, infection and the general risks of anaesthesia</li>
  <li>A raised hairline with some techniques</li>
</ul>
<p>Smoking and chewing tobacco slow healing and increase wound problems; you will be asked to stop well before surgery.</p>

<h2 id="which-suits-you">Which suits you?</h2>
<p>Botox may suit you if:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Your brows have dropped only slightly, mainly at the outer ends</li>
  <li>You would also like to soften frown lines</li>
  <li>You prefer minimal downtime and are happy to repeat treatment</li>
  <li>You want to see how a slight lift looks before considering surgery</li>
</ul>
<p>A surgical brow lift may suit you if:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Your brows sit noticeably low, with skin crowding the upper eyelids</li>
  <li>You constantly raise your brows to see clearly or keep your eyes open</li>
  <li>You want a longer-lasting change and accept the recovery and a scar</li>
  <li>One brow has dropped because of facial nerve weakness</li>
</ul>
<p>Both may form part of one plan — for example, surgery to reposition the brow and occasional toxin to manage frown lines. Some people with hollowing at the temples or under the brow may also benefit from <a href="/blogs/dermal-fillers-types-safety-what-to-expect/">dermal fillers</a>. And for some, the honest answer is that no treatment is needed at all.</p>
